lications."}], "places": [{"name": "Rome, Italy", "role": "Political Center"}, {"name": "New York City, USA", "role": "Economic Hub"}, {"name": "Athens, Greece", "role": "Cultural Birthplace"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: plutocracy", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "plutocracy"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q131708", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plutocracy"], "tags": ["form of government", "Political Parties & Organizations", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "plutocracy left a notable mark on Classical history, documented across 75 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Shaped institutional landscape and community development. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Plutocracy shaped political systems by concentrating power within the wealthy elite, leading to significant social unrest and reforms. Its implications continue to resonate today as societies grapple with wealth inequality and democratic representation.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-07T17:02:50Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#27098911410", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Society or system ruled and dominated by the small minority of the top wealthiest citizens", "newValue": "Plutocracy, a term first documented in the 17th century, describes a society wherein power is concentrated in the hands of the wealthiest individuals, profoundly influencing governance and social structures across various civilizations. The concept has roots in ancient societies, notably in Greece where the term was used to criticize the oligarchic rule by the wealthy. Throughout history, notable examples include the late Roman Republic, which saw the rise of wealthy patricians whose financial resources overshadowed the political power of the plebeians, and the Gilded Age in the United States (late 19th century), characterized by vast economic inequality and the political influence of industrial magnates like John D. Rockefeller and Andrew Carnegie.

In the late Roman Republic, the increasing concentration of wealth among the elite led to political corruption and social unrest, culminating in civil wars that transformed the Roman political landscape. The phrase "money talks" encapsulates how financial power dictated political alliances, with wealthy individuals like Crassus leveraging their fortunes to gain military and political dominance. Similarly, during the Gilded Age, industrialists not only amassed wealth but also exerted influence over policy decisions, leading to labor exploitation and economic disparities that provoked significant social movements and reforms.

